module gunbc.semantic_conformance

import std.types { String, NonEmptyStr, Bool, Int, List, list_length }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, declaration_ref_eq }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalAuthority }

// CONFORMANCE, NOT EQUIVALENCE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE MODULE.
//
// Equivalence asks whether two implementations agree. Conformance asks whether ONE implementation
// satisfies a NORMATIVE STATEMENT that exists independently of it. The two questions have the same
// shape and opposite epistemics: agreement between v1 and v2 is satisfiable by two implementations
// being wrong in the same way, and it is satisfiable BY CONSTRUCTION when the expected value was
// copied out of one of them. A value copied from v1 is not an expected value. Two implementations
// agreeing is not a definition.
//
// DESIGN section 3's replacement rule states the same thing from the migration side: Y never falls
// back to X, resolves through it, or derives its answer from it, while X may serve from history as
// an OFFLINE DIFFERENTIAL ORACLE. gunbc.interpreter_replacement_cut interpreter_cut_external_x
// already fixes v1's permitted roles at exactly ExternalDifferentialOracle and
// HistoricalMeasurementSource. This module is the carrier that makes that grant STRUCTURAL for the
// semantic question rather than remembered: an observation of v1 has no constructor by which it can
// inhabit the authority position, so "v1 is a probe, never the judge" is not a rule a reviewer
// enforces, it is a sentence that cannot be written.

// THE FIVE PRIMARY AUTHORITIES. Each one is a statement that can be true or false about an
// implementation WITHOUT running that implementation, which is the property that makes it normative.
// AuthoredExpectedOutcome is a human decision about what the program should do; AlgebraicLaw is a
// property of a structure the value inhabits (DESIGN section 4: operations come from inhabitance);
// MetamorphicRelation constrains how outputs must move when inputs move, and needs no reference
// output at all; RoundTripLaw is the one-grammar-read-in-both-directions identity; and
// ExternalTargetContract is the cited upstream specification, which is the deepest grounding
// available under DESIGN section 1's reduce-intersubjectivity-to-physics aim.
type PrimarySemanticAuthority
  = AuthoredExpectedOutcome { statement: NonEmptyStr }
  | AlgebraicLaw { law: NonEmptyStr, structure: DeclarationRef }
  | MetamorphicRelation { relation: NonEmptyStr }
  | RoundTripLaw { forward: DeclarationRef, backward: DeclarationRef }
  | ExternalTargetContract { contract: NonEmptyStr, citation: ExternalAuthority }

// THE THREE SUPPLEMENTARY OBSERVATIONS, IN THEIR OWN TYPE. This is the load-bearing construction of
// the module and it is a separate TYPE rather than three more arms of the coproduct above, because
// arms of one coproduct are interchangeable in every position that accepts the coproduct. A single
// nine-arm SemanticAuthority would make "the expected value is whatever v1 printed" a well-typed
// sentence, and the wall against it would then be a validator running somewhere else -- exactly the
// check DESIGN section 5 says to prefer construction over, and one satisfiable by editing the
// declaration while the realization still lies. With two disjoint types the invalid state has no
// constructor: BehaviorSubject.authority admits no value of this type at all.
type SupplementaryObservation
  = V2EvaluatorObservation { observed: NonEmptyStr }
  | LegacyV1Observation { observed: NonEmptyStr }
  | PreviouslyRecordedOutput { observed: NonEmptyStr, recorded_at_revision: NonEmptyStr }

// The expectation the authority yields. It is carried beside the authority rather than derived from
// it inside this module, because deriving it would require executing the law, and a carrier that
// executes its own normative statement has no independent referent left to compare an implementation
// against. What this module owns is that the expectation is JUSTIFIED BY a named producer -- see
// BehaviorSubject.authority_producer -- so the expectation is re-derivable rather than transcribed
// (DESIGN section 6: name the instrument, never transcribe its output).
type ExpectedOutcome
  = ExpectsValue { digest: NonEmptyStr }
  | ExpectsRefusal { diagnostic_identity: NonEmptyStr }
  | ExpectsEffectOrder { ordered: List<EffectReceiptEntry> }

type ObservedResult
  = ProducedValue { digest: NonEmptyStr }
  | RefusedWithDiagnostic { diagnostic_identity: NonEmptyStr }
  | NotRun

// v1's observation is typed as its own arm-carrying answer rather than as an ObservedResult field,
// so that NOT CONSULTING v1 is a state with a name. An optional whose absence means "we did not ask"
// and whose absence also means "it produced nothing" is the state-space conflation this repository
// rosters; these are different facts and only one of them is a measurement.
type LegacyObservation
  = LegacyRun { result: ObservedResult }
  | LegacyNotConsulted

type EffectReceiptEntry {
  operation: DeclarationRef
  ordinal: Int
}

type CoverageArea
  = CompilerClosureMechanism
  | RequiredV2TestClaim
  | PrimitiveOrAlgebraLaw
  | DiagnosticRefusalBehavior
  | SourceRootAndNameResolution
  | HostEffectAdmissionAndReceiptOrdering
  | Xl2NativeInterface

// A subject either HAS a primary authority or it is a discovered member of the coverage roster with
// none. The second arm is why SemanticAuthorityMissing is a reachable disposition rather than a
// decoration: BehaviorSubject cannot be constructed without an authority, so the missing case must
// enter the judgment as its own binding, discovered from the roster rather than authored as a
// half-filled subject.
type BehaviorSubject {
  subject_id: NonEmptyStr
  authority: PrimarySemanticAuthority
  authority_producer: DeclarationRef
  expected: ExpectedOutcome
  supplementary: List<SupplementaryObservation>
}

type SubjectAuthorityBinding
  = AuthorityBound { subject: BehaviorSubject }
  | AuthorityMissing { subject_id: NonEmptyStr, area: CoverageArea }

type DifferentialRecord {
  binding: SubjectAuthorityBinding
  native_v2: ObservedResult
  v2_evaluator: ObservedResult
  legacy_v1: LegacyObservation
  effect_receipt: List<EffectReceiptEntry>
}

// The nine total dispositions. Every arm names WHICH component is defective, because the remedies
// are different and a disposition that collapses them is total at the level examined and blind one
// level down. LegacyV1Defect is the arm that makes v1 a probe: v1 diverging from an authority the
// v2 routes both satisfy is a fact about v1, and it does not block anything.
type ConformanceDisposition
  = Conforms
  | NativeV2Defect { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | V2EvaluatorDefect { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | HostRealizationDefect { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | SemanticAuthorityMissing { subject_id: NonEmptyStr }
  | SemanticAuthorityConflict { subject_id: NonEmptyStr, detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | WitnessDefect { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | LegacyV1Defect { detail: NonEmptyStr }
  | Unclassified { detail: NonEmptyStr }

// Whether the v2 evaluator is still on the route the repository actually promotes. The brief this
// carrier serves tolerates V2EvaluatorDefect only once the evaluator is OFF that route, and the
// tolerance is therefore a fact about the route rather than about the defect -- so it is an input to
// the judgment, never a property a row can assert about itself.
type PromotedRoute
  = EvaluatorOnPromotedRoute
  | EvaluatorOffPromotedRoute

// COMPLETENESS IS STRUCTURAL, NOT A COUNT. Both arms can carry zero members and they answer the exit
// gate OPPOSITELY: an unmeasured area is a blocked gate, a measured-complete area with no members is
// an area that genuinely has none. A bare List cannot make that distinction and a note beside it
// cannot enforce it -- which is the whole reason an empty roster otherwise makes every guard over it
// vacuous, passing at zero equals zero.
type CoverageStanding
  = CoveragePopulationUnmeasured { area: CoverageArea, reason: NonEmptyStr }
  | CoveragePopulationMeasuredComplete {
      area: CoverageArea,
      members: List<DeclarationRef>,
      measured_revision: NonEmptyStr,
      producer: DeclarationRef
    }

fn observed_result_eq(a: ObservedResult, b: ObservedResult) -> Bool {
  match a {
    ProducedValue { digest: da } =>
      match b {
        ProducedValue { digest: db } => da == db
        _ => false
      }
    RefusedWithDiagnostic { diagnostic_identity: ia } =>
      match b {
        RefusedWithDiagnostic { diagnostic_identity: ib } => ia == ib
        _ => false
      }
    NotRun =>
      match b {
        NotRun => true
        _ => false
      }
  }
}

fn observed_satisfies_expected(expected: ExpectedOutcome, observed: ObservedResult) -> Bool {
  match expected {
    ExpectsValue { digest: d } => observed_result_eq(a: observed, b: ProducedValue { digest: d })
    ExpectsRefusal { diagnostic_identity: i } =>
      observed_result_eq(a: observed, b: RefusedWithDiagnostic { diagnostic_identity: i })
    ExpectsEffectOrder { ordered: _ } =>
      match observed {
        NotRun => false
        _ => true
      }
  }
}

fn observed_was_run(observed: ObservedResult) -> Bool {
  match observed {
    NotRun => false
    _ => true
  }
}

// ORDER IS COMPARED AT IDENTITY GRAIN, INCLUDING THE POSITION, rather than by walking two lists in
// parallel. A positional walk answers "the nth entries agree" and silently re-aligns when a single
// entry is inserted or dropped, so a receipt missing one effect can still match on every remaining
// pair. Carrying the ordinal on the entry makes position part of the identity being joined, which is
// what makes a reordered receipt a HostRealizationDefect rather than a shorter list that happens to
// agree.
fn effect_entry_eq(a: EffectReceiptEntry, b: EffectReceiptEntry) -> Bool {
  declaration_ref_eq(a: a.operation, b: b.operation) && a.ordinal == b.ordinal
}

fn effect_receipt_contains_entry(
  receipt: List<EffectReceiptEntry>,
  entry: EffectReceiptEntry
) -> Bool {
  fold(receipt, init: false, f: fn(acc, r) { acc || effect_entry_eq(a: r, b: entry) })
}

fn effect_receipts_agree(
  expected: List<EffectReceiptEntry>,
  receipt: List<EffectReceiptEntry>
) -> Bool {
  list_length(items: expected) == list_length(items: receipt)
    && fold(
         expected,
         init: true,
         f: fn(acc, e) { acc && effect_receipt_contains_entry(receipt: receipt, entry: e) }
       )
}

fn effect_receipt_matches_expected_order(
  expected: ExpectedOutcome,
  receipt: List<EffectReceiptEntry>
) -> Bool {
  match expected {
    ExpectsEffectOrder { ordered: ordered } =>
      effect_receipts_agree(expected: ordered, receipt: receipt)
    _ => true
  }
}

// v1 is consulted here and JUDGED NOWHERE. Its result is compared against the same authority every
// other route is compared against; it is never compared against the v2 routes, and no arm of
// classify_differential_record reads it before the v2 routes have already decided. That ordering is
// the executable form of "a probe, never the judge": deleting the legacy field cannot change any
// disposition except LegacyV1Defect.
fn legacy_diverges_from_authority(expected: ExpectedOutcome, legacy: LegacyObservation) -> Bool {
  match legacy {
    LegacyNotConsulted => false
    LegacyRun { result: r } => !observed_satisfies_expected(expected: expected, observed: r)
  }
}

fn classify_bound_record(
  subject: BehaviorSubject,
  record: DifferentialRecord,
  route: PromotedRoute
) -> ConformanceDisposition {
  if !observed_was_run(observed: record.native_v2)
      && !observed_was_run(observed: record.v2_evaluator) {
    WitnessDefect {
      detail: "no v2 route was observed: the record carries no measurement of the subject under judgment" as NonEmptyStr
    }
  } else {
    if !effect_receipt_matches_expected_order(
         expected: subject.expected,
         receipt: record.effect_receipt
       ) {
      HostRealizationDefect {
        detail: "ordered effect receipt does not match the effect order the authority requires" as NonEmptyStr
      }
    } else {
      if !observed_was_run(observed: record.native_v2) {
        Unclassified {
          detail: "the native v2 route was not observed, so no statement about it is available" as NonEmptyStr
        }
      } else {
        if !observed_satisfies_expected(
             expected: subject.expected,
             observed: record.native_v2
           ) {
          NativeV2Defect {
            detail: "the native v2 result does not satisfy the subject's primary authority" as NonEmptyStr
          }
        } else {
          if !evaluator_satisfies_or_is_excused(subject: subject, record: record) {
            V2EvaluatorDefect {
              detail: "the v2 evaluator result does not satisfy the subject's primary authority" as NonEmptyStr
            }
          } else {
            if !evaluator_observation_is_sufficient(record: record, route: route) {
              Unclassified {
                detail: "the v2 evaluator was not observed while it is still on the promoted route" as NonEmptyStr
              }
            } else {
              if legacy_diverges_from_authority(
                   expected: subject.expected,
                   legacy: record.legacy_v1
                 ) {
                LegacyV1Defect {
                  detail: "v1 diverges from the authority both v2 routes satisfy" as NonEmptyStr
                }
              } else {
                Conforms
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

fn evaluator_observation_is_sufficient(record: DifferentialRecord, route: PromotedRoute) -> Bool {
  match route {
    EvaluatorOffPromotedRoute => true
    EvaluatorOnPromotedRoute => observed_was_run(observed: record.v2_evaluator)
  }
}

fn evaluator_satisfies_or_is_excused(
  subject: BehaviorSubject,
  record: DifferentialRecord
) -> Bool {
  if !observed_was_run(observed: record.v2_evaluator) {
    true
  } else {
    observed_satisfies_expected(expected: subject.expected, observed: record.v2_evaluator)
  }
}

fn classify_differential_record(
  record: DifferentialRecord,
  route: PromotedRoute
) -> ConformanceDisposition {
  match record.binding {
    AuthorityMissing { subject_id: id, area: _ } => SemanticAuthorityMissing { subject_id: id }
    AuthorityBound { subject: s } =>
      classify_bound_record(subject: s, record: record, route: route)
  }
}

// CONFLICT IS A ROSTER FACT AND NOT A RECORD FACT, so it is derived where both sides are visible:
// one subject id carrying two authorities whose expectations differ is a conflict no single record
// can see. Same id and the SAME expectation is not a conflict but a duplicate authority row, which
// is a different defect with a different remedy and is left to the roster's own admission.
fn subjects_conflict(a: BehaviorSubject, b: BehaviorSubject) -> Bool {
  a.subject_id == b.subject_id
    && !expected_outcome_eq(a: a.expected, b: b.expected)
}

fn expected_outcome_eq(a: ExpectedOutcome, b: ExpectedOutcome) -> Bool {
  match a {
    ExpectsValue { digest: da } =>
      match b {
        ExpectsValue { digest: db } => da == db
        _ => false
      }
    ExpectsRefusal { diagnostic_identity: ia } =>
      match b {
        ExpectsRefusal { diagnostic_identity: ib } => ia == ib
        _ => false
      }
    ExpectsEffectOrder { ordered: oa } =>
      match b {
        ExpectsEffectOrder { ordered: ob } => effect_receipts_agree(expected: oa, receipt: ob)
        _ => false
      }
  }
}

fn disposition_blocks_cutover(
  disposition: ConformanceDisposition,
  route: PromotedRoute
) -> Bool {
  match disposition {
    Conforms => false
    LegacyV1Defect { detail: _ } => false
    V2EvaluatorDefect { detail: _ } =>
      match route {
        EvaluatorOffPromotedRoute => false
        EvaluatorOnPromotedRoute => true
      }
    NativeV2Defect { detail: _ } => true
    HostRealizationDefect { detail: _ } => true
    SemanticAuthorityMissing { subject_id: _ } => true
    SemanticAuthorityConflict { subject_id: _, detail: _ } => true
    WitnessDefect { detail: _ } => true
    Unclassified { detail: _ } => true
  }
}

type ExitGateVerdict
  = ExitGateOpen
  | ExitGateBlocked { blocking_dispositions: Int, unmeasured_areas: Int }

fn coverage_standing_is_measured(standing: CoverageStanding) -> Bool {
  match standing {
    CoveragePopulationUnmeasured { area: _, reason: _ } => false
    CoveragePopulationMeasuredComplete {
      area: _,
      members: _,
      measured_revision: _,
      producer: _
    } => true
  }
}

// THE GATE READS BOTH DENOMINATOR AND NUMERATOR, and the first half is the one that is usually
// missing. Counting blocking dispositions over a roster nobody has measured passes at zero, so the
// gate is open exactly when every coverage area is MeasuredComplete AND no judged record blocks. An
// area whose measurement has not been performed keeps the gate shut with a nonzero unmeasured count,
// which is the honest state rather than a green.
fn conformance_exit_gate(
  dispositions: List<ConformanceDisposition>,
  route: PromotedRoute,
  coverage: List<CoverageStanding>
) -> ExitGateVerdict {
  fold(
    coverage,
    init: fold(
      dispositions,
      init: ExitGateOpen,
      f: fn(acc, d) {
        if disposition_blocks_cutover(disposition: d, route: route) {
          match acc {
            ExitGateOpen => ExitGateBlocked { blocking_dispositions: 1, unmeasured_areas: 0 }
            ExitGateBlocked { blocking_dispositions: b, unmeasured_areas: u } =>
              ExitGateBlocked { blocking_dispositions: b + 1, unmeasured_areas: u }
          }
        } else {
          acc
        }
      }
    ),
    f: fn(acc, standing) {
      if coverage_standing_is_measured(standing: standing) {
        acc
      } else {
        match acc {
          ExitGateOpen => ExitGateBlocked { blocking_dispositions: 0, unmeasured_areas: 1 }
          ExitGateBlocked { blocking_dispositions: b, unmeasured_areas: u } =>
            ExitGateBlocked { blocking_dispositions: b, unmeasured_areas: u + 1 }
        }
      }
    }
  )
}

// THE CEILING OF THE DISJOINTNESS CLAIM, STATED RATHER THAN CLAIMED GREEN. The wall that a
// SupplementaryObservation cannot inhabit BehaviorSubject.authority is structural: the invalid state
// has no constructor, which is DESIGN section 4b rung 4. Its RED is therefore a source fixture the
// compiler must REFUSE, not a Bool a witness can return -- and a total function over
// PrimarySemanticAuthority returning true on all five arms would be permanently green by
// construction, carrying no information and worse than absent because it would be cited as coverage.
// So no such witness is written here. The next-rung trigger is a refusal fixture harness able to
// hand this module's declarations to the compiler as source and assert the located refusal; until
// that harness exists the claim is established by inspection of the two type declarations above and
// by the absence of any conversion between them in this module.
data supplementary_disjointness_ceiling_note: String = "The disjointness of PrimarySemanticAuthority and SupplementaryObservation is rung 4 (structurally impossible) for the class 'an observation of a second implementation is used as the expected value'. It is NOT witnessed by an executing Bool in this lane, deliberately: the discriminating RED is a source fixture the compiler must refuse, and a witness asserting a property no authorable input can violate would be a decoration. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, naming the capability rather than an artifact: a compiler-refusal fixture harness sufficient to hand arbitrary .dag source containing BehaviorSubject { authority: LegacyV1Observation { .. } } to the acceptance path and assert a located typed refusal. When that capability lands, this note dissolves and the fixture is enrolled as the permanent regression control."
